//! tools/fixtures/microkernel_probe.rye -- the smallest honest answer to the Microkernel Target arc's Equinox 3 question: can the Rye
//! compiler lower to a capability-microkernel target with today's real toolchain? Probe, not a port.
//!
//! THE QUESTION, AS SEATED. external-research/20260817-185851 names it and leaves it open: "Can Zig target seL4 / Genode today? ... This is
//! the external-research question the request named, and it should be answered against real toolchain versions, not from memory." The
//! `20260819` recenter restates it as still open and leading Equinox 3.
//!
//! WHAT THIS PROBES, and what it deliberately does not. A capability microkernel asks three things of a root task: that it be FREESTANDING
//! (no host OS beneath it), that it carry NO LIBC, and that it call a C ABI which traps into the kernel. This module is those three things
//! and nothing more. It calls `cap_invoke` from tools/fixtures/microkernel_cap_stub.c -- OUR OWN capability-shaped stub -- and folds the
//! result so the witness can prove every argument crossed the C boundary intact.
//!
//! CLEAN ROOM. No seL4 or Genode source, header, or line is fetched, copied, or reproduced anywhere in this probe; both are copyleft and
//! studied for design concepts only (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md). The stub's names are ours. What is proven here is the mechanism
//! the whole family requires, which is exactly the part a toolchain either supports or does not.
//!
//! Built by tools/microkernel_zig_target_probe_witness.rish for riscv64, aarch64, and x86_64, each freestanding.

const cap_word = u64;
const cap_handle = u64;

/// The capability invocation, reached across the C ABI. In a real system this traps into the kernel; here it reaches our own stub, which
/// is the same call shape either way -- and the call shape is what the toolchain has to be able to emit.
extern fn cap_invoke(capability: cap_handle, label: cap_word, word: cap_word) cap_word;

/// A bounded fold over three invocations, so the probe proves more than that one call links: it proves arguments and returns survive the
/// boundary across repeated calls, with every value named and no allocation anywhere.
export fn probe_capability_path() cap_word {
    var total: cap_word = 0;
    var i: cap_word = 1;
    // invariant: the loop is bounded by a named constant, never by an input.
    const rounds: cap_word = 3;
    while (i <= rounds) : (i += 1) {
        total +%= cap_invoke(i, i +% 1, i +% 2);
    }
    return total;
}

/// The entry symbol a freestanding image needs. A real root task would receive its initial capability space here; the probe only proves
/// the image links and the symbol is reachable with no host runtime beneath it.
export fn _start() callconv(.c) noreturn {
    _ = probe_capability_path();
    while (true) {}
}
